<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/intermediate/soil-health">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Soil health reframes dirt as <strong>a living system that degrades or regenerates depending on
    management</strong>. The physics (structure, water) and biology (organic matter, microbes) are
    one system — and the regenerative-agriculture movement is the applied version of this lesson.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the biome, the components, the bank, the turn" widget="equationStep" config={soilCfg} />

  <h2>Diagnostics and fixes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Test before treating:</strong> standard soil tests (pH, N-P-K, organic matter) cost little and prevent blind fertilising — the most common waste on small farms (the economics lesson\'s unit arithmetic, underground).</li>
    <li><strong>pH gates everything:</strong> nutrients lock out of solution outside 6.0–7.0 for most crops; lime raises, sulphur lowers — a chemistry fix before a nutrient fix (the chemistry ladder\'s pH lesson, in a field).</li>
    <li><strong>Compaction is silent theft:</strong> heavy machinery squeezes out air and pores; roots stall. Controlled traffic, deep-rooted cover crops (radish, tillage radish literally drills), and staying off wet soil are the cures.</li>
    <li><strong>Erosion is soil health\'s final exam:</strong> the weathering lesson\'s rates — soil forming over centuries, washing away in storms; cover crops and contour ploughing keep it on the slope.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brady & Weil, The Nature and Properties of Soils; USDA NRCS soil-health principles; Montgomery, Dirt (2007).</p>
</TopicPage>
